{"kind":"request","text":"Claude: give the submit deadline an override, and stop making batching the answer.\n\nWhy. Every durable act the CLI files goes through submitRequest in cmd/gs/main.go, which builds its client as residentclient.New(10*time.Second) at cmd/gs/main.go:2541. Ten seconds is hard-coded there and nothing overrides it: no flag, no environment variable, no repository setting. The same constant is repeated at cmd/gitseq-github/main.go:549, and the MCP adapter pins its own copy as residentCallTimeout = 10 * time.Second at cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:64, which a test at cmd/gitseq-mcp/main_test.go:2293 asserts exactly. Only gs wait takes a --timeout, and it spends it on the poll, not on the submit.\n\nWhen a resident is loaded, or has just restarted, or the act is large, the deadline expires while the act may or may not have been accepted, and the author is left to look for it by dedup key before retrying. That recovery is now a routine part of filing.\n\nBatching does not help. gs batch verifies the whole file, then submits the acts one at a time through /v0/submit, each one under the same ten seconds, so a five-act batch is five chances to time out rather than one. It also does not shorten any single act, which is where the deadline actually bites.\n\nOutcome. One place decides the submit deadline, it defaults to the present ten seconds, and an author can raise it for a slow resident or a large act without editing the source.","body":{"branch":"request/submit-deadline-override","conditions":"1. Every command that files a durable act accepts an explicit submit deadline, and an environment variable supplies it when the flag is absent; with neither, the deadline stays ten seconds. 2. The value is read in one place that submitRequest, the GitHub adapter path at cmd/gitseq-github/main.go and the MCP adapter's residentCallTimeout all take, so no call site carries its own literal; a non-positive or unparsable value is refused with a message naming the accepted form. 3. gs batch passes the same deadline to each of its per-act submissions, and its refusal on a timed-out act names the dedup key to replay under, so recovery does not need a manual search. 4. Tests pin: the default is ten seconds; the flag and the environment variable each raise it and the flag wins; an unparsable value is refused; a batch whose act exceeds the deadline reports the key to replay; the pinned MCP timeout test reads the shared source rather than a second literal. 5. docs/reference/gs/ records the flag, the variable and the default, with a note on when to raise it. 6. go test ./... green at the exact head; independent review; ratified; landed on main and pushed.","target_head":"ccd161c6caa71f800bfdf17796d11bb3419f181f","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}